WEBVTT

00:00:01.000 --> 00:00:06.200
Drivine is a graph database client, for
Kotlin and Java, TypeScript and Python.

00:00:06.360 --> 00:00:10.673
It works with graph databases
like Neo4j, FalkorDB and Memgraph,

00:00:10.673 --> 00:00:14.920
and with relational databases
like Postgres, Oracle and SQLite.

00:00:15.160 --> 00:00:16.360
It works at two levels.

00:00:16.855 --> 00:00:20.582
The persistence manager runs
your queries, written in Cypher,

00:00:20.582 --> 00:00:22.415
even on a relational database.

00:00:22.535 --> 00:00:25.975
And the stateless graph object
manager maps your graph to objects.

00:00:27.640 --> 00:00:30.200
But it's not a traditional
object graph mapper.

00:00:30.360 --> 00:00:34.520
Those start from one big model of your
domain, and generate queries from it.

00:00:34.760 --> 00:00:38.454
The model doesn't fit every use
case, so you fetch too much,

00:00:38.454 --> 00:00:40.240
or too little, and it's slow.

00:00:40.240 --> 00:00:45.265
Drivine takes its cue from GraphQL,
and from composition over inheritance.

00:00:45.505 --> 00:00:49.768
You compose small, reusable fragments
into a view for each use case,

00:00:49.768 --> 00:00:52.465
and Drivine loads
it in a single statement.

00:00:52.625 --> 00:00:53.665
That's why it's fast.

00:00:55.193 --> 00:00:56.793
So why a graph at all?

00:00:56.873 --> 00:01:00.953
A graph lets you think in networks,
and bring graph data science to them.

00:01:01.353 --> 00:01:03.353
And it's a great fit for AI apps.

00:01:03.353 --> 00:01:06.313
Take retrieval augmented
generation, or RAG.

00:01:06.473 --> 00:01:10.525
Before a language model answers, you
search your own documents for the passages

00:01:10.525 --> 00:01:13.758
closest to the question,
and give them to the model as context.

00:01:14.238 --> 00:01:17.054
Usually those passages are
chunks in a vector store,

00:01:17.054 --> 00:01:21.277
each cut off from its document, its
neighbours, and the things it talks about.

00:01:21.678 --> 00:01:24.613
So the model gets fragments,
without their context.

00:01:25.013 --> 00:01:27.813
In a graph, a match comes
with what it's connected to.

00:01:27.973 --> 00:01:29.493
That's graph RAG.

00:01:29.493 --> 00:01:33.594
But it usually means picking a graph
database first, and staying on it.

00:01:33.594 --> 00:01:37.151
Drivine aims to give you the cutting-edge
experience of working in the graph

00:01:37.151 --> 00:01:40.473
paradigm, with whatever database you
have, in the language you work in.

00:01:42.073 --> 00:01:45.913
Here's graph RAG as Embabel does it,
in its graph RAG module.

00:01:45.913 --> 00:01:48.872
Embabel is an agent framework for the JVM.

00:01:48.872 --> 00:01:51.536
A chunk is a fragment,
with a vector index,

00:01:51.536 --> 00:01:53.953
and a view adds what it's connected to.

00:01:53.953 --> 00:01:57.585
The stateless graph object manager
loads the nearest chunks by vector,

00:01:57.585 --> 00:02:00.128
with everything the view
names, in one statement.

00:02:00.608 --> 00:02:04.947
Essentially the whole of Embabel's
graph RAG uses the graph object manager,

00:02:04.947 --> 00:02:08.477
and Drivine takes care of writing
the queries and statements:

00:02:08.477 --> 00:02:12.528
in each platform's flavour of Cypher,
or in SQL or SQL/PGQ, as needed.

00:02:14.140 --> 00:02:16.060
The database is configuration.

00:02:16.540 --> 00:02:21.755
The same code runs on Neo4j,
FalkorDB and Memgraph, and on Postgres,

00:02:21.755 --> 00:02:25.820
Oracle and SQLite,
which don't run Cypher themselves.

00:02:26.300 --> 00:02:30.533
There, Drivine keeps the graph
in two tables and writes SQL.

00:02:30.933 --> 00:02:34.053
Move from one to another,
and nothing in your code changes.

00:02:35.593 --> 00:02:40.616
You can write plain openCypher on any
of these stores, even the relational ones:

00:02:40.616 --> 00:02:44.321
on Postgres, Oracle and SQLite,
Drivine compiles it to SQL,

00:02:44.321 --> 00:02:47.272
and gives back the same
rows as a graph engine.

00:02:47.673 --> 00:02:52.724
But the object manager saves you time:
it uses your types, writes the queries,

00:02:52.724 --> 00:02:54.084
and maps the results.

00:02:54.324 --> 00:02:56.665
So use it wherever
it makes your life easier,

00:02:56.665 --> 00:02:59.164
and fall back to Cypher
for your custom queries.

00:03:00.540 --> 00:03:01.820
Here it is running.

00:03:01.820 --> 00:03:03.846
Guide is an Embabel agent that answers

00:03:03.846 --> 00:03:06.140
questions from Embabel's
own documentation.

00:03:06.460 --> 00:03:09.788
Its store is built on Drivine's
stateless graph object manager,

00:03:09.788 --> 00:03:12.060
and runs on any
of the supported databases.

00:03:12.867 --> 00:03:16.227
Could you show me what,
an agent looks like?

00:03:16.227 --> 00:03:19.907
Show me some code in Kotlin
for a simple Embabel agent.

00:03:19.907 --> 00:03:22.867
Here's a simple Embabel
agent written in Kotlin.

00:03:22.867 --> 00:03:24.307
Jasper for a horoscope.

00:03:24.307 --> 00:03:28.047
The agent Graph RAG is one way
graphs help an AI app.

00:03:28.207 --> 00:03:31.288
You can also build an agent's
memory and conversation history,

00:03:31.288 --> 00:03:34.767
knowledge graphs it can reason over,
and the domain model it works in.

00:03:35.087 --> 00:03:39.093
For example, Embabel's chat store
keeps an agent's conversations,

00:03:39.093 --> 00:03:44.272
with a vector for every message, on any of
the supported databases, through Drivine.

00:03:44.272 --> 00:03:47.905
And DICE builds knowledge graphs
of propositions an agent reasons over,

00:03:47.905 --> 00:03:49.952
on any of Drivine's supported databases.

00:03:51.700 --> 00:03:53.438
Drivine also ships with Studio,

00:03:53.438 --> 00:03:56.579
an optional tool that comes
in handy during development.

00:03:57.060 --> 00:04:02.100
Run ad hoc Cypher, or load your Drivine
models and see what each view brings back.

00:04:02.100 --> 00:04:06.340
Drivine is open source, for Kotlin
and Java, TypeScript and Python.

00:04:06.955 --> 00:04:10.875
Build AI apps on the database you already
run, and keep the freedom to move.

00:04:10.875 --> 00:04:14.371
Drivine is the database
integration layer for Embabel,

00:04:14.371 --> 00:04:18.190
the AI agent framework for the JVM,
created by Rod Johnson,

00:04:18.190 --> 00:04:20.651
who also created the Spring Framework.

00:04:20.651 --> 00:04:23.131
Drivine was created by Jasper Blues.
